1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,986
This is the engineering behind SESSION: orders that fill

2
00:00:05,046 --> 00:00:09,632
at the NYSE open or close, at a price verified on-chain.

3
00:00:09,692 --> 00:00:14,030
Two programs, a shared core, one keeper, and receipts

4
00:00:14,090 --> 00:00:15,440
anyone can check.

5
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:20,381
The bell oracle, session-bell, only keeps prints: it

6
00:00:20,441 --> 00:00:24,499
never holds funds. The exchange, session-cross, holds

7
00:00:24,559 --> 00:00:28,229
the escrow and does the pricing, the auction and

8
00:00:28,289 --> 00:00:32,348
settlement. Both share one Rust core for the calendar

9
00:00:32,408 --> 00:00:36,700
and the arithmetic. Every step after placing an order is

10
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:40,663
permissionless. The keeper is a convenience, not an

11
00:00:40,723 --> 00:00:41,440
authority.

12
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:45,112
A print arrives as one transaction.

13
00:00:45,172 --> 00:00:49,546
Instruction zero is Solana's Ed25519 precompile, and its

14
00:00:49,606 --> 00:00:53,506
offsets point at our instruction, where the signed

15
00:00:53,566 --> 00:00:55,882
message starts at byte twelve.

16
00:00:55,942 --> 00:01:00,316
The program hands that message to Pyth's verifier, which

17
00:01:00,376 --> 00:01:04,434
checks the signer is trusted and unexpired, and then

18
00:01:04,494 --> 00:01:08,869
parses every byte itself. The close is the last price in

19
00:01:08,929 --> 00:01:12,987
the seconds before 4 PM; the open is the first after

20
00:01:13,047 --> 00:01:17,263
9:30. Regular session only, tight confidence, strictly

21
00:01:17,323 --> 00:01:21,698
better prices replace worse ones, and a price stamped in

22
00:01:21,758 --> 00:01:23,440
the future is refused.

23
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:28,684
Orders collect in escrow until two minutes before the

24
00:01:28,744 --> 00:01:33,029
bell, then the book freezes. Once the print is final,

25
00:01:33,089 --> 00:01:37,373
the cross prices one raw token at the print times the

26
00:01:37,433 --> 00:01:41,226
mint's own multiplier, read from its Token-2022

27
00:01:41,286 --> 00:01:45,734
extension. It cancels instead if the multiplier changes

28
00:01:45,794 --> 00:01:49,997
near the bell. Buyers and sellers net at that price.

29
00:01:50,057 --> 00:01:54,587
The larger side's remainder goes to a two-minute auction

30
00:01:54,647 --> 00:01:59,177
on a 101-step fee ladder. The clearing fee is the lowest

31
00:01:59,237 --> 00:02:03,112
ask that covers the need, and the last bucket is

32
00:02:03,172 --> 00:02:07,538
rationed. Everything consumed rounds up and everything

33
00:02:07,598 --> 00:02:11,883
received rounds down, so the escrow can never pay out

34
00:02:11,943 --> 00:02:13,440
more than it holds.

35
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:18,725
The arithmetic is property-tested, ten thousand cases a

36
00:02:18,785 --> 00:02:23,030
property. The programs run in LiteSVM against the real

37
00:02:23,090 --> 00:02:27,494
NVIDIA token, captured from mainnet at its real address,

38
00:02:27,554 --> 00:02:31,799
and prices verified by Pyth's real verifier binary: 19

39
00:02:31,859 --> 00:02:34,270
oracle cases, 9 exchange cases.

40
00:02:34,330 --> 00:02:38,735
A fuzzer runs 300 random crosses across more than a year

41
00:02:38,795 --> 00:02:43,040
of trading days, and every balance must match the math

42
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:47,345
to the atom. The TypeScript SDK decodes the very bytes

43
00:02:47,405 --> 00:02:51,331
the Rust wrote. And a headless browser places real

44
00:02:51,391 --> 00:02:54,440
orders on devnet and checks every atom.

45
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:59,018
Failure modes run in public, at a real bell.

46
00:02:59,078 --> 00:03:03,114
We paused an NVIDIA-shaped token before the open.

47
00:03:03,174 --> 00:03:07,378
The cross still priced and cleared, the keeper paid

48
00:03:07,438 --> 00:03:11,892
every quote leg alone, and the tokens waited until the

49
00:03:11,952 --> 00:03:16,238
issuer resumed. On a second token we scheduled a new

50
00:03:16,298 --> 00:03:20,502
multiplier five minutes after the bell, and pricing

51
00:03:20,562 --> 00:03:24,932
refused to guess: it cancelled and refunded everyone,

52
00:03:24,992 --> 00:03:27,440
whole. Both escrows read zero.

53
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:32,786
At the bell, the keeper quotes both sides of the cross

54
00:03:32,846 --> 00:03:37,150
on Jupiter, for the real NVIDIA token on mainnet, and

55
00:03:37,210 --> 00:03:41,678
writes the quotes into the very transaction that prices

56
00:03:41,738 --> 00:03:45,960
the cross. A receipt shows that comparison only from

57
00:03:46,020 --> 00:03:50,323
that transaction, and only if the keeper named in the

58
00:03:50,383 --> 00:03:54,440
manifest signed it. Anyone else's memo is ignored.

59
00:03:55,400 --> 00:04:00,272
Agents get five MCP tools: status, quote, receipt, place

60
00:04:00,332 --> 00:04:05,028
and cancel. The two that write are capped per order in

61
00:04:05,088 --> 00:04:09,079
code and refused on mainnet unless a flag says

62
00:04:09,139 --> 00:04:13,659
otherwise. A Blink builds the same order for whoever

63
00:04:13,719 --> 00:04:17,974
clicks. It checks the balance before asking for a

64
00:04:18,034 --> 00:04:20,440
signature, and holds no key.

65
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:24,727
Don't take the page's word for any of it.

66
00:04:24,787 --> 00:04:28,692
Every receipt links the print's transaction, its

67
00:04:28,752 --> 00:04:32,575
signature check, and the memo beside the price.

68
00:04:32,635 --> 00:04:37,118
The programs, the keeper and the tests are open source.

69
00:04:37,178 --> 00:04:38,440
Check the chain.
